E2 - the goodwill decay rate at which the strict reversal breaks (REG-002 E2)
==============================================================================
  the paper's ladder, R = [0.3, 0.26667, 0.15, 0.03333]
  strictly decreasing (the tau = -1 claim): True

  closed form: delta_3* = K*alpha/(1+K) with K = R_2/(1-phi_3) = 0.187500
  delta_3*                      = 0.007895
  implied half-life             = 87.5 periods
  the paper assumes delta_3     = 0.002000  (half-life 346.2)
  margin                        = 0.005895
  bisection check               = 0.007895   (|closed - bisect| = 0.00e+00)
  SEVERE     the closed form for the crossing rate agrees with bisection to 1e-9

  at delta_3 = 0.00829 (5% above the crossing):
    R = [0.3, 0.26667, 0.15, 0.15899]
    strictly decreasing: False   Kendall tau = -0.67
  SEVERE     a goodwill decay rate barely above the crossing destroys the tau = -1 reversal
  Witness is the paper's own calibration, where the ladder IS strictly
  decreasing -- so the check is reading the goodwill rate and nothing else.

  REG-002 E2 threshold: delta_3* < 0.010 -> knife edge.  delta_3* = 0.007895  -> FIRES

==============================================================================
E3 - lumpy, not slow (REG-002 E3)
==============================================================================
  REG-002 section 3.1 declared this extension BEFORE it was written: LayeredFirm
  takes a scalar entropy_rate; E3 needs a per-period sequence. The extension is a
  strict generalisation and its reproduction of LayeredFirm is itself a check.

  constant-sequence reproduction of LayeredFirm: max |gap diff| = 0.000e+00
  SEVERE     the varying-decay extension reproduces LayeredFirm on a constant sequence
  Witness is the same comparison at a DIFFERENT constant rate, which must differ.

  smooth class : delta = 0.01 every period
  lumpy class  : delta = 0.200 with probability 0.05, else 0
                 -> identical MEAN decay rate 0.0100, phi = 0.2

  R at the mean rate (closed form)      : 0.200000
  realised deferral ratio, lumpy path   : 0.260659  (se 0.001861, 2000 paths)
  lumpy / smooth                        : 1.3033
  in delta-equivalent terms, the lumpy class defers like a smooth class at delta = 0.01229
  SEVERE     a lumpy decline defers MORE than a smooth one at the same mean rate
  Witness is the smooth class measured against itself, which is 1.0 by
  construction and cannot exceed the tolerance.

  So 'unscheduled' proxied as 'slow' is wrong TWICE over: the mean rate need not
  be small, and at any given mean rate lumpiness RAISES the deferral measure.
  Goodwill's delta-equivalent under this lumpy path is 0.01229, against the
  paper's assumed 0.00200 and the crossing rate 0.00789.
  Above the crossing: True

==============================================================================
E4 - the disclosed-numbers ladder (REG-002 E4)
==============================================================================
  Two rungs need no inference. ASC 360 and ASC 350-30-50 make firms DISCLOSE
  useful lives for property and for finite-lived intangibles. Under REG-002
  section 3.2's stated bridge, a disclosed life L gives a write-down rate 1/L.
  That bridge is an ASSUMPTION -- but a falsifiable one about a published number,
  which is strictly better located than an inference from an ABSENCE.

  property, plant and equipment : lives 10-40y -> delta_0 in [0.0250, 0.1000]
  finite-lived intangibles      : lives 3-20y -> delta_1 in [0.0500, 0.3333]
  the paper's table asserts       delta_0 = 0.030 > delta_1 = 0.020  (lives 33y and 50y)

  fraction of the disclosed rectangle INSIDE the model's domain (delta < alpha): 0.0%
  of that admissible part, the first rung RISES in : 0.0%
  as a share of the whole rectangle               : 0.0%

  the boundary is exact: rung 0->1 falls iff  (1-phi_1)*g(delta_1) < (1-phi_0)*g(delta_0),
  g(d) = d/(alpha-d).  With phi = (0.80, 0.60) that is g(delta_1) < 0.5*g(delta_0),
  i.e. delta_1 < alpha*delta_0 / (2*alpha - delta_0).
  At the paper's delta_0 = 0.030 the boundary sits at delta_1 = 0.02143
  and the paper picks delta_1 = 0.020 -- inside by 0.00143, i.e. a life of 46.7y against the 50y assumed.
  SEVERE     the paper's first rung sits within a hair of its own boundary
  Witness is the same margin computed for tier 2's rate, which is not near it.

  REG-002 E4's registered falsifier is a share of the ADMISSIBLE rectangle,
  and at alpha = 0.05 that rectangle is EMPTY. The registered test is vacuous
  here -- not passed, not failed. Reported as such, and evaluated below at an
  alpha where it has a domain. That substitution is an EXTENSION of REG-002 E4,
  not the registered test, and is labelled so in the manuscript.
  SEVERE     at the paper's calibration the disclosed rectangle lies entirely outside the model's domain
  Witness is the same rectangle against a recognition rate of 0.35, which contains
  it -- so the check is reading alpha and not the rectangle.

  recognition rate needed for HALF the disclosed rectangle to be admissible : 0.1915  (a catch-up half-life of 3.3 periods)
  recognition rate needed for ALL of it                                     : > 0.3333
  the paper's alpha                                                         : 0.0500

  EXTENSION, alpha = 0.35: admissible share 100.0%, first rung RISES in 99.7% of it
  boundary in general form: the rung falls iff delta_1 < alpha*delta_0 / (2*alpha - delta_0),
  which tends to delta_0/2 as alpha grows. Disclosure amortises finite-lived
  intangibles over SHORTER lives than property, i.e. delta_1 > delta_0 -- so the
  rung cannot fall anywhere a filing actually puts a firm.
  SEVERE     wherever the question has a domain at all, the first rung of the paper's table runs the other way on disclosed useful lives
  Witness caps delta_1 at the boundary curve, where the rung cannot rise -- so a
  check that passed for both would not be reading the curve.

==============================================================================
E5 - what governs the direction, FITTED (REG-002 E5)
==============================================================================
  Section 4.4's decomposition: a rung rises iff
      dlog(1-phi) + dlog(delta) - dlog(alpha-delta) > 0
  Define, per ladder, the DESIGN BUDGET as the mean design term per rung and the
  DELTA LEVERAGE as the mean absolute combined delta contribution per rung.

  fitted logit P(design fails) = +0.7801 + +1.5812 * log(leverage/budget)
     se                        =  0.0684          0.0810
     z on the slope            =  +19.5
  crossover (P = 0.5) at leverage/budget = 0.6106

  THE DESIGN RULE, and it is a fitted number, not an asserted one: a phi-ordered
  cross-section is more likely than not to recover its own ordering only while the
  ladder's per-rung delta leverage stays below 0.61 times its design
  budget. The paper's own ladder sits at 2.58.
  SEVERE     the fitted slope is positive and many standard errors from zero
  Witness is the identical fit on a PERMUTED outcome: z = 0.23, so the check is reading the ladder and not the design matrix.

