E6 - the alpha-delta boundary, which the disclosed numbers walk into (REG-002 E6)
==============================================================================
  R = (1-phi)*delta/(alpha-delta) has a pole at delta = alpha and is NEGATIVE
  beyond it. The paper's calibration keeps delta <= 0.030 under alpha =
  0.05. Disclosed intangible lives of 20 years or shorter cross it.

     delta   d/alpha   log10 ratio t=400       t=800   closed form   settles
     0.010      0.20              -0.699      -0.699        0.2000      True
     0.030      0.60               0.079       0.079        1.2000      True
     0.045      0.90               0.801       0.851        7.2000     False
     0.049      0.98               1.129       1.348       39.2000     False
     0.051      1.02               1.330       1.732      -40.8000     False
     0.060      1.20               2.513       4.358       -4.8000     False
     0.100      2.00               9.597      18.989       -1.6000     False
     0.200      4.00              29.881      59.735       -1.0667     False
  (log10 of the gap ratio, because past the pole it leaves the page: at delta =
   0.200 it reaches 10^69 by t = 400 and 10^140 by t = 800.)

  delta < alpha : ratio settles     (1.1997 -> 1.2000, change 2.88e-04)
  delta > alpha : ratio does NOT    (3949647364.0531 -> 9749821445631545344.0000, change 9.75e+18)
  SEVERE     no steady-state deferral ratio exists once the decay rate exceeds the recognition rate
  Witness is the sub-critical case asked the same question, which settles.

  So section 4.4's measure is defined only where the recognition rate exceeds the
  economic decay rate. That is not a calibration detail: it is a statement about
  WHICH FIRMS the section speaks to, and it belongs in the section.

